{{smaller block|He ''tiff'd'' his punch, and went to rest.
{{right|''W. Combe'', Dr. Syntax's Tours, i. 5.}}}}

II. ''intrans.'' To lunch. [Anglo-Indian.]

'''tiff'''<sup>2</sup> (tif), ''n.'' [A reduction of ''tift''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', or from the related ''tiff'': see ''tiff''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'' Cf. ''tift''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. also ''tip''<sup>3</sup>.] 1. A draught of liquor; a "drop": as, a ''tiff'' of brandy.

{{smaller block|What say you to a glass of white wine, or a ''tiff'' of punch, by way of whet?
{{right|''Fielding'', Amelia, viii. 10.}}
Sipping his ''tiff'' of brandy punch with great solemnity.
{{right|''Scott'', Guy Mannering, xi.}}}}

2. Thin or small beer. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|That too shall quickly follow, if
It can be rais'd from strong or ''tiff.''
{{right|''Brome'', Answer to his University Friend.}}}}

'''tiff'''<sup>3</sup> (tif), ''v. i.'' [Prob. orig. 'sniff' in anger, and so ult. identical with ''tiff''<sup>2</sup>, < Norw. ''teva'' = Icel. ''thefa'', sniff: see ''tiff''<sup>2</sup>.] To be in a pet; be peevish or quarrelsome.

{{smaller block|Poor Mincing ''tift'' and ''tift'' all the Morning.
{{right|''Congreve'', Way of the World, ii. 4.}}
She ''tiff'd'' at Tim, she ran from Ralph.
{{right|''Landor'', New Style.}}}}

'''tiff'''<sup>3</sup> (tif), ''n.'' [< ''tiff''<sup>3</sup>, ''v.''] A petty quarrel or misunderstanding; a slight pet, or fit of peevishness.

